I am providing missing information for this gap. I verified the contradiction firsthand and here is the fix with verbatim anchors.

EDIT_SITE (the gap has none, which is why it cannot be grounded or composed):
repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/concept-usage-record.ts

THE CONTRADICTION, both read at 2026-08-29:
Header comment (~line 45) claims:
  'a placeholder/empty trace_id gets a unique\n  // synthesized fallback so each usage event still keys distinctly.'
The code below (~line 68) states and does the OPPOSITE:
  'No real trace attribution — do NOT synthesize a usage row. Synthetic\n  // autonomous_backfill_* rows inflated concept relevance (usage credit must\n  // come only from a real execution trace that loaded the concept).'

THE CODE IS RIGHT AND MUST NOT CHANGE. Synthesizing a fallback trace_id is exactly what inflated concept relevance; usage credit has to come from a real execution trace. Only the stale header clause is wrong.

THE FIX — one contiguous string replacement in that one file. Replace this exact text:

  // failed — skip the write entirely; a placeholder/empty trace_id gets a unique
  // synthesized fallback so each usage event still keys distinctly.

with:

  // failed — skip the write entirely. A placeholder/empty trace_id is ALSO skipped: an
  // earlier version synthesized a fallback id here, and those synthetic autonomous_backfill_*
  // rows inflated concept relevance, so usage credit now comes only from a real execution
  // trace that loaded the concept. See the trace_id branch below, which this sentence used to
  // contradict.

DO NOT 'fix' this by making the code synthesize a fallback to match the comment — that would reintroduce the relevance inflation the code comment documents. The comment is the stale half.

VERIFY BY CONSEQUENCE: the header and the trace_id branch state the same policy, and concept-usage-record still returns skipped:true with recorded:false for a placeholder trace_id.
